WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.874
Redis keeps all its data in memory, and memory is wiped when a program stops.

2
00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:13.316
So now and then, Redis writes a copy of everything to a file on disk: a snapshot.

3
00:00:13.533 --> 00:00:17.463
But Redis runs every command on one thread, one path of work.

4
00:00:17.463 --> 00:00:21.973
So how can it copy gigabytes to disk without making every client wait?

5
00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:27.657
The plain way is the command SAVE: the main thread writes the file itself.

6
00:00:27.657 --> 00:00:33.444
In the lab, a million payments, 83 megabytes, took 2,065 milliseconds to save.

7
00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:40.963
A client that asked for one key 200 milliseconds in waited 1,761 milliseconds for its answer.

8
00:00:40.963 --> 00:00:47.663
Nothing else ran, because the only thread that runs commands was busy writing a file.

9
00:00:47.833 --> 00:00:51.125
With 25 gigabytes, that pause would last minutes.

10
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:54.823
Think of a café that keeps today's orders on a whiteboard.

11
00:00:54.823 --> 00:00:59.072
Wipe the board and they're gone, so now and then you take a photo of it.

12
00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:05.268
SAVE is a photographer who makes the staff stop writing until he has copied the whole board by hand.

13
00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:09.806
So let a helper copy the board while the staff keep writing?

14
00:01:09.806 --> 00:01:16.588
Then the copy mixes moments: payment 5 from ten o'clock, payment 900,000 from two seconds later.

15
00:01:16.767 --> 00:01:20.552
A refund could land in the file without the payment it refunds.

16
00:01:20.552 --> 00:01:25.420
We need both: someone else does the copying, and the copy still shows one moment.

17
00:01:25.633 --> 00:01:28.689
Redis's answer is BGSAVE, a background save.

18
00:01:28.689 --> 00:01:36.676
The main thread calls fork: a request to the kernel, the core of the operating system, to copy the running program.

19
00:01:36.867 --> 00:01:40.133
The original is the parent, and the copy is the child.

20
00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:44.368
The child will write the file, and the parent goes on serving clients.

21
00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:48.780
But copying 25 gigabytes of memory would take seconds too.

22
00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.104
So what does fork actually copy?

23
00:01:51.104 --> 00:01:52.267
Pause and guess.

24
00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:56.579
Almost nothing.

25
00:01:56.579 --> 00:02:06.223
Memory is handed out in pages, blocks of 4 kilobytes, and each program has a page table, the kernel's map from its addresses to pages.

26
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.091
Fork copies only the map.

27
00:02:08.091 --> 00:02:10.864
Parent and child point at the same pages.

28
00:02:10.864 --> 00:02:16.276
In the café, the photographer gets a list of the board's panels; no panel moves.

29
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:22.181
The child writes every key to a temporary file, and the parent serves clients at full speed.

30
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.380
Now a client changes payment 5 to refunded.

31
00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:28.707
It lives on page B, which the child still needs.

32
00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:33.125
So the kernel first copies page B, and gives the copy to the parent.

33
00:02:33.125 --> 00:02:35.548
The parent writes refunded on its copy.

34
00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.769
The child still sees page B as it was, with payment 5 settled.

35
00:02:39.769 --> 00:02:43.116
Its picture stays frozen at the moment of the fork.

36
00:02:43.333 --> 00:02:48.352
That's copy-on-write: a page is copied only when someone is about to change it.

37
00:02:48.352 --> 00:02:55.720
In the café, a waiter about to write on a panel first swaps in a fresh copy, and the photographer keeps the old one.

38
00:02:55.900 --> 00:03:00.771
Everyone's happy, except the memory chips, which now hold one page twice.

39
00:03:01.067 --> 00:03:05.088
When the child finishes, it renames its file to dump.rdb.

40
00:03:05.088 --> 00:03:12.003
A rename happens in one step, so a crash halfway leaves the old snapshot, never half of a new one.

41
00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:17.305
Then the child exits, and the old page B, which only the child used, is freed.

42
00:03:17.533 --> 00:03:24.175
In the lab, forking 83 megabytes took 6 to 31 milliseconds, instead of a two-second stop.

43
00:03:24.467 --> 00:03:27.899
Two costs remain, and both fall on the parent.

44
00:03:27.899 --> 00:03:33.271
The fork still copies the page table: 8 bytes for every 4 kilobyte page.

45
00:03:33.271 --> 00:03:41.553
A 24 gigabyte Redis copies 48 megabytes of table, at about 10 to 25 milliseconds per gigabyte on most machines.

46
00:03:41.767 --> 00:03:45.762
And every page the parent changes during the save gets copied.

47
00:03:45.762 --> 00:03:49.499
In the lab, a few hundred updates copied 3 to 5 megabytes.

48
00:03:49.499 --> 00:03:54.075
On a server that writes heavily, it can come close to twice the memory.

49
00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:58.293
So keep Redis's memory limit well below the machine's memory.

50
00:03:58.567 --> 00:04:01.210
And a snapshot is only as new as its fork.

51
00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:07.945
With the default rules, a busy server saves about once a minute, so a crash loses about a minute of writes.

52
00:04:08.167 --> 00:04:11.769
Kill Redis now, and the restart loads dump.rdb.

53
00:04:11.769 --> 00:04:16.521
Payment 5 is settled again; the refund after the fork is gone.

54
00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:20.228
For a cache, that's fine: it refills from the database.

55
00:04:20.228 --> 00:04:22.729
For the café's payment notes, it isn't.

56
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:28.678
In Java and Kotlin, BGSAVE is one command, and Redis answers as soon as it has forked.

57
00:04:28.678 --> 00:04:33.893
Its stats then tell you how long the fork took, and how much the kernel copied.

58
00:04:34.367 --> 00:04:38.072
So, how does Redis photograph its memory without stopping?

59
00:04:38.072 --> 00:04:38.647
It forks.

60
00:04:38.647 --> 00:04:46.696
The child gets the picture, the parent keeps working, and the kernel copies a page only when the parent is about to change it.

61
00:04:47.033 --> 00:04:48.126
One quick check.

62
00:04:48.126 --> 00:04:51.951
During a save, the parent changes payment 5 three times.

63
00:04:51.951 --> 00:04:55.162
How many copies of page B does the kernel make?

64
00:04:55.162 --> 00:04:56.255
Pause and think.

65
00:04:59.500 --> 00:04:59.782
One.

66
00:04:59.782 --> 00:05:06.895
After the first copy, page B prime belongs to the parent alone, so the next writes go straight to it.

67
00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:12.141
Next, to lose less than a minute, the café's notebook: a log of every change.
